Z||000004||Notchit||01-23-2001||09:10 PM||||I have an 85 GT, I love it!<BR>although...the only things i have to compare to it are an 87 Ranger, and a 84 olds station wagon  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Seein Red<BR>'85 Fiero GT<BR>ICQ#: 56708070<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Outside_side2(sml2).jpg"> <BR>_________________________||||reg||10
Z||000005||Predator||01-23-2001||09:30 PM||||I have an 85 GT and I enjoy it as well. I would say it really depends on how the car was taken car of more than the year it was made. I've seen beautiful 88's and junk 88's. I have seen the same for 85's as well. Sometimes you get lucky and find a real great car that has been well maintained, and sometimes you don't. I have owned practically new cars that were junk, and I have owned a 1971 Torino GT that I could have driven to he*l and back, and never would have worried about it breaking down along the way  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<BR>Just my opinions though. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||6
Z||000006||BN Boomer||01-23-2001||10:12 PM||||I've got an 85 GT as well. I don't believe there are any problems unique to this year or model. The V-6 engine in mine tired out and refused to run past 115,000 miles, which is why I got a pretty good deal on it.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/boomers_fiero_files/85gt2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||LottaBallsCamaro||01-23-2001||10:43 PM||||This is all great info but I'm wondering about the Fiero in general, how long do they run, How high is insurence, How hard are they really to work on?||||reg||4
Z||000008||Old Lar||01-23-2001||11:02 PM||||The highest milage coupe I've seen had 419000 miles on it original engine, 3rd or 4th clutch.  I know of a V6 that has over 200000 miles on it and it still runs great.  I bought an 88 GT with 54000 and the engine was shot.  So, the car's life expectancy is dependent on how well the car was cared for.<P>Ease to work on is in your experience on the car.  If you have the room, the experience and tools, you car drop the engine out the bottom in an hour and it is easy to work on.  As with any car ove 10 years old just about anything can be wrong.<P>Insurance is age dependent and driving record. young and dumb: two seater sports car = high insurance.<P>When they were new, they were afforable fun cars to drive.  Today they are still fun to drive.||||reg||3
Z||000009||jch82||01-23-2001||11:02 PM||||The fiero is just like any other car. When you buy a car (fiero or not) the odds of it breaking down are the same. It has to do with how well the previous owners cared for the car. As for insurance, the GT's will cost more than any other model. The other models are pretty cheap because the fiero was considered a commuter car by insurance companies.<P>Josh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||1FieroGTRacingInc||01-23-2001||11:11 PM||||I have a 85GT also....  lol   It's a GREAT part's car!!!  lol  <BR>I also have a 87GT,a 86 SE...<BR> I love working on mine...  Get a Manual at your local Auto Part's Store!!!  It's worth EVERY single dime!!!!   They are pretty easy to work on... I mean be patient and THINK ABOUT THE Fiero like you are working on a PLASTIC CAR MODEL!!!! lol...  Am I CLOSE Guy's and Gal's???  God , I love these Fiero's....<BR>By the way ...  WELCOME TO THE FORUM... <P>------------------<BR>1FieroGTRacingInc.||||reg||1
